course: biology
lesson: 56
title: How plants grow: seeds, light, water and air
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A plant is a food factory: in its green leaves it takes light from the Sun, water from the ground and carbon dioxide from the air, and turns them into sugar, giving out oxygen as it works; this is called photosynthesis.
explain: Put a broad bean in a jam jar with wet paper towel on the windowsill: it drinks, swells and splits, a root pushes down, a shoot pushes up, and it lives on the food packed inside the seed until its first leaves open.
explain: Here is the big surprise: a tall oak that grew from a tiny acorn is made mostly from the air, because the carbon in carbon dioxide becomes wood, while the soil gives only water and small amounts of minerals.
explain: Plants also move, slowly: a cress plant on the windowsill leans towards the light, and a sprouting potato in a dark cupboard sends out long pale shoots searching for it.
explain: Cartoons show plants eating soil with roots like spoons, and carnivorous plants swallowing people; real plants make their own food from light, air and water, and no plant can eat a person.
fact: A seed takes in water, swells and splits; first a root grows down, then a shoot grows up, and this is called germination.
fact: A seed carries its own food store, so it can grow before it has leaves; roots grow down and shoots up whichever way the seed lies, because they sense gravity.
fact: Shoots bend towards light because cells on the shady side grow longer; plants grown in the dark become tall, pale and weak, stretching to look for light and staying pale because they cannot make green chlorophyll without it.
fact: In photosynthesis, leaves use light, water and carbon dioxide to make sugar and give off oxygen; the green substance chlorophyll catches the light.
fact: Almost all the oxygen we breathe was made by plants and tiny algae in the sea.
fact: In the 1600s Jan van Helmont grew a willow in a pot for five years; it gained about 74 kg while the soil lost only a few grams.
fact: Water inside plant cells pushes outwards and keeps stems firm, like air in a balloon; without enough water the plant wilts.
fact: Soil gives plants water, grip for their roots and minerals such as nitrogen; without minerals plants grow small and yellow.
fact: Most seeds need warmth as well as water and air to germinate, and a date palm seed about 2000 years old was grown in Israel.
fact: Plants grow mainly at the tips of their shoots and roots, and some bamboos can grow nearly a metre in a day.
fact: A Venus flytrap shuts when an insect touches two trigger hairs within about 20 seconds; it lives in poor boggy soil and gets nitrogen from insects; even the biggest meat-eating plants catch only insects and, rarely, a frog or mouse.
fact: A potato is a swollen underground stem full of starch, and its sprouts are new shoots; green parts of potatoes can be poisonous.
fact: In a fair test you change only one thing and keep everything else the same.
exercise: What is germination? | A seed swelling and splitting as its root and shoot start to grow | A flower bud opening its petals in the sunshine | A leaf turning yellow in autumn | A fruit falling from a tree
exercise: In the jam jar, which grows first out of the broad bean? | The root, pushing down | The leaves, opening up to the light | The flower, right at the top | The fruit, inside the bean
exercise: How can a bean grow before it has any leaves to make food? | It uses the food stored inside the seed | It slowly eats the wet paper towel around it | It takes sugar from the glass jar it sits in | It sucks food up from the windowsill below
exercise: Why does a cress plant lean towards the window? | Cells on the shady side grow longer, bending it towards the light | The wind from the open window blows it over | It is trying to escape from the pot | The window glass pulls it with static
exercise: What do a sunflower's green leaves make in photosynthesis? | Sugar, giving out oxygen | Soil, giving out clean water | Pollen, giving out puffs of smoke | Seeds, giving out carbon dioxide
exercise: What three things do leaves use to make food? | Light, water and carbon dioxide | Soil, oxygen and darkness at night | Sugar, salt and air | Worms, water and soil
exercise: Where does most of the material of a tall oak come from? | Carbon dioxide from the air, plus water | Soil eaten up slowly by the roots over the years | Rain alone | The acorn it grew from
exercise: Why does a drooping pot plant stand up again after watering? | Water fills its cells and they press out firmly again | The water is heavy and pushes it up | The plant gets a fright from the cold water and jumps up straight | The soil gets hard and holds it
exercise: Why does a bean grow better in compost than in sand? | Compost holds water and gives minerals the plant needs | Sand is too sharp for roots | Beans hate the colour of sand | Compost is warmer because it is dark, and beans only like the warm
exercise: Why do seeds in a cold garage wait until spring to sprout? | Most seeds need warmth to germinate | They are scared of the dark in the garage | They need the smell of flowers nearby | Seeds sprout only on Sundays, never in the week
exercise: Where does a plant do most of its growing? | At the tips of its shoots and roots | In the middle of its stem, near the soil | Only in its leaves | Only in its flowers
exercise: How does a Venus flytrap know to snap shut? | An insect touches two trigger hairs within about 20 seconds | It sees the fly coming with a row of tiny eyes along the edge | It hears the buzzing | It shuts every minute in case
exercise: What is a sprouting potato in the cupboard? | A swollen stem full of starch, growing new shoots | A root that has gone bad | A giant seed with a hard brown shell, like a nut | A fruit that fell off the plant
exercise: In the two cress pots, one in a cupboard and one on the windowsill, what is being tested? | Whether cress needs light | Whether cress needs water | Whether cress needs soil | Whether cress needs warmth
exercise: What happens to cress grown in a dark cupboard? | It becomes tall, pale and weak | It grows short, dark and strong | It does not change at all | It turns bright red
exercise: In a fair test, how many things should you change? | Only one, keeping everything else the same | As many as possible, so you learn more at once | Two at a time | None at all
test: mia reckons plants eat soil to grow. right? | No, plants make their food from light, air and water | Yes, the soil goes up the stem | Yes, but only at night | No, plants eat insects mostly
test: Van Helmont's willow gained about 74 kg in five years, but the soil lost only a few grams. Where did the weight come from? | Mostly from the air and the water | From the soil, which grew back by itself | From the pot | From worms in the soil
test: What would happen if you turned the bean jar upside down after the root had started? | The root would bend to grow down again and the shoot up | The root would keep growing straight up into the air of the jar | The bean would stop growing for ever | The root and shoot would swap places
test: What would happen if you turned the cress pot round every day? | It would grow straighter, as no side stays in the shade | It would grow in a spiral | It would get dizzy and die | It would bend away from the light and lean towards the dark wall instead
test: Where does almost all the oxygen we breathe come from? | Plants and tiny algae in the sea | Volcanoes and hot springs | The soil | Clouds and rain
test: In a cartoon a giant plant swallows the hero whole. Can any real plant eat a person? | No, the biggest catch insects and, rarely, a frog or mouse | Yes, deep in the jungle there are man-eating trees that grab people | Yes, the Venus flytrap when it is fully grown | No, because people are poisonous to plants
test: Why might a plant that lives in poor soil start eating insects? | To get nitrogen the soil cannot give it | Because it is too lazy to make its own sugar from light | To stop insects eating it first | Because it cannot get water
test: Why does a potato in a dark cupboard grow long pale shoots? | The shoots stretch to look for light and cannot turn green | The cupboard is too cold | Potatoes always grow pale | It is trying to reach the other potatoes in the bag to share their water
test: My plant's all floppy, no wait, it's the lettuce on the table. why's it gone like that? | It has lost water, so its cells went soft | It is too cold | It needs more salt sprinkled on it | It has been nibbled by slugs in the night
test: If you painted a line on a tree trunk, would it move up as the tree grows? | No, trees grow taller at the tips, so the mark stays at that height | Yes, it moves up a little each year as the trunk stretches upwards | Yes, it moves to the top within a year | No, it moves down into the ground
test: How would you test whether cress needs warmth to grow? | Grow two pots, one warm and one cold, keeping light and water the same | Grow one pot in a cold dark cupboard and one warm on the windowsill | Water one pot with warm water and one with nothing | Grow one pot and watch it carefully every day
exam: Identify the process by which green plants manufacture sugar from light, water and carbon dioxide. | Photosynthesis | Germination of the seed | Pollination of the flower | Respiration of the leaf
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the origin of the mass of a mature tree? | It derives chiefly from atmospheric carbon dioxide and water | It is derived principally from soil drawn up by the roots over many years | It is derived principally from rainfall alone | It is wholly contained within the original seed
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning cress cultivated in total darkness. | It becomes elongated, pale and weak | It becomes short, dark and vigorous | It remains wholly unaltered | It acquires a red colouration
exam: It is given that an investigator wishes to ascertain whether warmth is necessary for germination. Determine the variable to be altered. | Temperature alone, all other conditions being held constant | Temperature together with the supply of water and the quantity of light | Light, temperature and soil simultaneously | No variable whatsoever
